 solution  it's the political negotiated solution  to this it will continue and continue if  they only try military means alright so  are you encouraged by these crisis talks  that are going to take place this  weekend do you think do you hope they'll  achieve this political solution that  you're looking for well I'm hopeful I  mean the only way out is that Russia and  the US have joint leadership to guide us  out of this that Iran Saudi Arabia and  the Gulf countries who are also on each  side of this war stop bringing fuel to  the fire but help int us into political  negotiations and in the meantime we need  humanitarian access to the people we  haven't been able to reach eastern  aleppo with any significant aid since  the end of June the medical crisis could  not be worse the remaining six  functioning hospitals have nearly all of  them been bombed there our latest  figures I have are even worse than yours  well over 400 dead now well over 100  dead children well over 1,300 injured  were working around the clock now to try  to get evacuation of wounded get medical  supplies in and get even new personnel  more medical personnel in but was still  unsuccessful because the party are not  giving us the pause we need and the  fighting and all the permits we need to  entice deliver do you feel that the  international community as a whole has  failed the people of Syria absolutely  we've all failed  we'll all failed in this I feel very  strongly we failed the besieged areas of  Aleppo we've gotten more aid in this  year by far compared to last year but  then again the people are bombed every  day and night they are not content to  get blankets and  and a little bit more more food they  need an end to the bombardment these are  civilians here we have hospitals that  that are being targeted it's a war crime  to target hospitals and they have been  repeatedly targeted in eastern Aleppo  and by the way also by mortars coming  into West Aleppo which is government  controlled 